# Omnivax CLI Documentation

The `omnivax` command-line utility provides developers with the tools to manage authentication, register and check models, push local weights and configs, and execute batch deployments from model hubs.

---

## 📦 Installation

To install the CLI locally as an editable package inside your virtual environment:

```bash
pip install -e cli/
```

Verify that the installation was successful by running:
```bash
omnivax --help
```

---

## 🔑 Authentication Commands

The CLI stores session tokens and configuration settings locally (in your XDG home directories).

### Login
Log in to your Omnivax developer account:
```bash
omnivax auth login
```
*Optional parameters:*
* `--url <URL>`: Set a custom Supabase Project URL.
* `--key <KEY>`: Set a custom Supabase Anon/Publishable Key.

### Logout
Clear your active authentication session and tokens:
```bash
omnivax auth logout
```

### Whoami
Display details about the currently authenticated session:
```bash
omnivax auth whoami
```

---

## 🛡️ API Key Management

Long-lived API keys can be used to run automated scripts and continuous integration pipelines without manual logins.

### Create an API Key
Generate a new API key with a custom description name:
```bash
omnivax auth keys create "My Production CI"
```
> [!IMPORTANT]
> Make sure to copy the displayed API key immediately! You will not be able to retrieve it again.

### List API Keys
View all your generated API keys:
```bash
omnivax auth keys list
```

### Revoke an API Key
Permanently delete an API key by its unique ID:
```bash
omnivax auth keys revoke <KEY_ID>
```

---

## 🔑 External Model Hub Credentials

Securely configure your API tokens and keys for Hugging Face and Kaggle. Stored credentials are encrypted on the backend and dynamically injected into Celery worker tasks during hub downloads.

### Set Credentials
Configure or update your credentials:
```bash
omnivax auth credentials set --source huggingface
```
*Optional parameters:*
* `-s, --source <huggingface|kaggle>`: Specify the platform.
* `-t, --token <TOKEN>`: Pass the Hugging Face access token directly (will prompt securely if omitted).
* `-u, --username <USERNAME>`: Pass the Kaggle username (required for Kaggle).
* `-k, --key <KEY>`: Pass the Kaggle API key (will prompt securely if omitted, required for Kaggle).

### List Credentials
View the status of your configured model hub credentials:
```bash
omnivax auth credentials list
```
*Note: Tokens and API keys are masked for security.*

### Delete Credentials
Revoke and delete the stored credentials for a platform:
```bash
omnivax auth credentials delete <huggingface|kaggle>
```

---

## 🧠 Model Catalog Commands

Interact with the Omnivax Model Catalog to inspect, upload, and deploy models.

### List Models
View all registered models on the platform:
```bash
omnivax models list
```

### Get Model Details
Fetch detailed metadata, accuracy metrics, and status of a specific model:
```bash
omnivax models get <MODEL_ID>
```

### Push a Local Model
Deploy a local model directory directly to the platform. 
```bash
omnivax models push <MODEL_ID> --name "<Model Name>" --dir <DIRECTORY_PATH>
```
*   **Automatic Scanning**: The CLI automatically scans the target directory to locate your weights file (e.g. `weights.pth`) and a standard `config.json` file.
*   **Custom Config Option**: You can explicitly pass a configuration JSON string or local path using the `--config` (or `-c`) parameter.

---

## 🚀 Model Hub Deployment (HF / Kaggle)

Deploy and download pre-trained weights from model repositories asynchronously using Celery and Redis.

### Deploy from Model Hub
Deploy a single public model from Hugging Face or Kaggle:
```bash
omnivax models deploy-hub <MODEL_ID> --name "<Model Name>" --source <huggingface|kaggle> --repo-id "<REPO_ID>"
```
*Options:*
* `--filename <FILE>`: Specify a single file to download instead of the entire repository snapshot.
* `--framework <pytorch|onnx|tensorflow>`: Specify model architecture framework (default: `pytorch`).
* `--config <JSON_OR_PATH>`: Pass custom class mappings or configurations.

### Massive Batch Deploy
Trigger bulk model downloads and registrations via a batch configuration file:
```bash
omnivax models batch-deploy <PATH_TO_CONFIG_JSON>
```
*   **Batch Config Schema**: A JSON file containing a list of `items` to deploy:
```json
{
  "items": [
    {
      "model_id": "hf_vit_disease",
      "name": "ViT Disease Classifier",
      "source": "huggingface",
      "repo_id": "google/vit-base-patch16-224",
      "framework": "pytorch",
      "class_names": ["healthy", "rust", "mold"]
    }
  ]
}
```
*   **Real-time Dashboard**: When executed, the CLI starts a live-updating console panel showing download status, testing logs, and activation status for each model.

---

## 🧪 Diagnostic Inference Commands

### Run a Prediction
Execute a diagnostic classification request using a registered model:
```bash
omnivax run predict <MODEL_ID> <IMAGE_PATH>
```
*Example:*
```bash
omnivax run predict efficientnet_b0_v1 ./test_leaf.jpg
```
This prints a styled diagnostic report with disease labels and confidence levels.
